# Laravel PHPUnit mock Request

When testing a controller method that relies on `Illuminate\Http\Request`, it is common to encounter friction when mocking the object. The core issue often stems from a misunderstanding of how the `has()` method behaves versus how the underlying data structure works, and consequently, how to configure the mock to reflect that behavior accurately.

In the scenario where you are writing a PHPUnit test for a controller, you might have code that checks for the existence of specific input fields like `username` or `email`. If your mock setup causes `$request->has('username')` to return the entire payload array instead of a boolean, your logic flow will break. This happens because the expectation set on the mock is too broad and does not account for the conditional nature of the `has()` method.

The specific problem arises from how you configure the expectations for the `get()` and `has()` methods. In your test setup, you defined an expectation for `has` that returns `$requestParams` whenever it is called. The logic inside your controller expects a boolean resultâ`true` if the key exists, `false` otherwise. By returning the full array, you are effectively telling the controller that the check passed, but with incorrect data types that can lead to unexpected type coercion errors or logic failures downstream.

To fix this, you need to distinguish between the raw data retrieval and the existence check. The `get()` method should indeed return the collection of parameters, but `has()` must return a boolean value based on whether the requested key is present in that collection. You cannot simply pass the same array back for every call; you must inspect the input arguments passed to the mock to determine what to return.

Here is how you can adjust your mock expectations to handle this correctly:

```php
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use PHPUnit\Framework\MockObject\MockObject;

public function testIndex()
{
    $request = $this->createMock(Request::class);

    $requestParams = [
        'username' => 'test',
        'email'    => 'test@test.com'
    ];

    // Configure 'all()' to return the full payload
    $request->method('all')
        ->willReturn($requestParams);

    // Configure 'get()' to return specific values based on the key provided
    $request->expects($this->any())
        ->method('get')
        ->with('username')
        ->willReturn('test')
        ->willReturnCallback(function ($key) use ($requestParams) {
            return $requestParams[$key] ?? null;
        });

    // Configure 'has()' to check if the key exists in the original array
    $request->expects($this->any())
        ->method('has')
        ->withConsecutive(
            ['username'],
            ['email']
        )
        ->willReturnCallback(function ($key) use ($requestParams) {
            return array_key_exists($key, $requestParams);
        });

    // Now the logic inside your controller will work as expected:
    // if ($request->has('username')) will correctly return true (bool)
}
```

The key takeaway here is that mocking a complex interface like `Request` requires mimicking its internal logic, not just its signature. When you use `willReturn($requestParams)` for the `has()` method, you are bypassing the existence check entirely. Instead, you should pass the `$requestParams` array into your expectation configuration and use a callback or specific `with()` constraints to return `true` only when the requested key matches an existing one in that array.

This approach ensures that if you later remove the `username` key from `$requestParams` during another test case, the mock will automatically return `false` for `has('username')`, maintaining the integrity of your test assertions without needing to manually change every expectation string. This pattern is essential for writing robust unit tests that validate both positive and negative scenarios involving conditional logic in your controllers.
